  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a single bypass project in one local Japanese city, which may limit generalizability to other urban contexts or larger network expansions.",
      "what": "This study evaluated the impact of a new bypass parallel to a national highway in a local Japanese city using GPS-based journey records of 141 million trips before and 167 million trips after the opening. Using propensity score matching across 18 data-screening scenarios, the analysis found that the bypass reduced average route-level travel times by 1.02 to 2.75 minutes and planning times by 0.55 to 3.21 minutes. However, the effects on travel time variability, measured via standard deviation and buffer time, were not robust across the different scenarios.",
      "why": "It provides empirical, GPS-derived evidence that road investments can reliably reduce average travel times and planning times, while demonstrating that measured improvements in travel time variability are highly sensitive to how temporal data is screened."
    }
  },
